          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I had flu two weeks ago and I am still exhausted. Is prolonged tiredness afterwards a concern, and what does guidance describe about antivirals and vaccination?",
          "answer": "Based on the information I found, I can't say whether your tiredness is a concern. The sources I searched don't describe how long tiredness after flu normally lasts, so I can't tell you whether two weeks is typical. A clinician who knows your history is the right person to ask.\n\nThe general influenza reference (*Influenza*, General clinical reference) says that most otherwise healthy people are managed with rest, fluids and symptom relief. It also notes that influenza is linked to a short-term rise in the risk of heart attack and stroke in the weeks after infection. It describes a pattern of getting worse after several days of improvement as worth recognising. Because of that link, a clinician should check any tiredness that persists or comes with new symptoms.\n\nOn antivirals and vaccination, the *Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment* guideline (National immunization advisory committee and infectious diseases society guidance) describes routine yearly vaccination for people aged 6 months and older who have no contraindication. It describes the usual aim as vaccinating before the end of October in the northern hemisphere, and says vaccination later in the season is still worthwhile while the virus is circulating. The passage I received did not cover the timing for antiviral treatment or the groups it is recommended for. Those details are said to be in the influenza guideline note, but that text was not returned, so I can't report them.\n\nSeek urgent medical care now if you have chest pain or pressure, or a sudden, severe headache. The emergency guidance in this system covers those symptoms, and they need prompt assessment whatever their cause. My check of your description found no emergency pattern, but that does not rule out a serious problem.\n\nConsider booking an appointment with a doctor or nurse soon if your tiredness is not improving, is getting worse, or you have any new symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
